WEBVTT

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.280
With the rapid development of Hyper Technology came the Labor...

00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:15.380
a multi-purpose, humanoid construction machine whose use...

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:17.290
spread into almost every field.

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:23.330
However, this gave rise to a new threat to society: Labor crime.

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:31.300
The Tokyo Metro Police founded the Special Vehicles, Section 2...

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:36.430
within the Security Division to combat these ever-growing crimes.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:42.480
This was the beginning of the SV2 Patrol Labor... Patlabor... units.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:03.080
kimi ni ikarete METAMETA MECHAMEKA gurui
You really threw a wrench into my works

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:09.560
HAATO BIITO wo awaserya sore de ikisou yo
If I can get my heartbeat under control, I think I'll make it

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:15.270
kimi to atashi wa miraiha koi no TEKUNISHAN
We're futurists, you and me; technicians of love

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:20.910
takaku hayaku suki yo ki mama ni
Higher, faster, I'll love you if I want to

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:27.460
METARU BODII no hadazawari sae
I think I'll get to like

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:33.400
atsuku kanjite kuru kuse ni narisou
The way I get flushed at the touch of your metal body

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:39.690
CHACHI na Imagination koeta jigen de
Oh, don't stop this gravity-defying feeling

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:46.580
juuryokuihan ah nukeru Feeling tomenai de
That goes way beyond mere imagination

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:53.100
kimi ni ikarete METAMETA MECHAMEKA gurui
You really threw a wrench into my works

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:59.550
PITAri kakarerya PIKAA kireru ARUFONSU
When I hold him tight, my Alphonse can do anything

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:05.180
kimi to atashi wa miraiha koi no TEKUNISHAN
We're futurists, you and me; technicians of love

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:13.760
sora mo umi mo kyou mo ashita mo Nothin' impossible
Anytime, anywhere, nothing's impossible

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:17.720
Nothing's impossible

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:22.330
Sorry that took so long...

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:54.230
Unit 2, prepare to fire!

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:56.700
Fire!

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.910
Whoa.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:07.350
Holy <i>shit.</i>

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:11.220
This'll do. This'll do <i>real</i> nicely.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:16.990
That thing's just too dangerous; I don't think we should use it.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.430
You can't be serious!

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:23.230
Do you know how hard it was to scrape together those spare parts?

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:25.770
It took me 4 months and 16 days...

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:27.670
- You stay out of this.
- But...

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:31.640
Chief! You have a phone call from the Investigations Division.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:33.770
What? The <i>Investigations</i> Division?

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:37.840
I'm Inspector Matsui, with Criminal Investigations.

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:40.720
I know this is sudden, but it's about <i>that.</i>

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:56.830
With that recent cutting of an undersea cable used by the...

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:00.700
Babylon Project and an unmanned buoy disappearing soon afterwards...

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:02.800
the Maritime Safety Agency borrowed it...

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:05.070
from a civilian outfit to investigate the cause.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:09.710
I asked you here because nobody at HQ knows how to operate it.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:14.220
Why is someone from Investigations doing a job for the MSA?

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.950
Actually, I was working on another case...

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:20.190
trying to find a car that had apparently fallen into the bay.

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:21.990
Well, it's a strange case.

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:23.060
Strange?

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.460
A couple had parked their car...

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:28.260
on a pier near the Battery to make out.

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:31.430
The boyfriend went to buy a drink, and he says that when he came back...

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:35.800
the car... and the woman inside it - had vanished into thin air.

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:41.880
The woman was found unconscious nearby on the beach this morning...

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.680
but whatever she saw, it gave her such a shock...

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:47.350
that she's in no shape to give a statement.

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:52.720
Now here's the problem: there were tire tracks left on the pier.

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:53.990
They went <i>sideways.</i>

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:55.220
"Sideways"?

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:59.160
It was like a big hand had grabbed it like this...

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:02.130
and pulled it into the sea with enormous force.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.370
I've spoken with them about my investigation...

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:12.840
so I was wondering if I could trouble you to help out.

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:19.280
We don't have time to tag along afterwards, but all right.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:21.620
Thank you.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:25.980
Maritime Safety Agency

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:31.160
So you can actually catch fish out here?

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:35.100
Heck, I caught a bunch of creepy giant flounders just the other day.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:37.070
And now there's <i>nothing?</i>

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:42.330
I've been fishing here for ten years.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:44.910
This is the first time anything like this has happened.

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:59.780
Let go! Let go of me! Let <i>go</i> of me, all right?

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:01.260
Enough, already!

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:04.190
If you keep this up, we'll have you arrested!

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:05.590
Go home, okay?

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:09.300
You'll regret this someday! And when you do, it'll be too late!

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:10.460
Don't you understand?

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:12.470
Oh, <i>sure.</i>

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:25.850
- What was <i>that</i> all about?
- Beats me.

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:28.350
Hey, sorry that took so long!

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:33.260
Sorry about that. The Old Man and I won't be going back today.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:36.060
- I'm really sorry.
- So you're gonna pilot that, Shige?

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:37.860
- Yep!
- You're lucky.

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:39.990
Hey, I'll trade with you.

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:43.800
But the Old Man's gonna be looking over your shoulder the whole time.

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:45.770
Pass.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:02.480
40... 45...

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:04.150
50...

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:05.420
Touchdown.

00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:18.470
Visibility's bad. Can't be more than ten meters.

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:21.170
Could you head towards the Battery first?

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:22.500
Sure thing.

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:51.530
Tokyo Bay is awfully big. This is gonna be a tough job.

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:53.770
Well, if we don't return this Labor tonight...

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:56.040
they'll charge us an overdue fine.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:58.040
Hold on. What was that to the left?

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:01.810
Oh, <i>that.</i>

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:05.320
That's one of those excavation trenches left from construction.

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:07.390
They're not uncommon in the bay.

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:10.010
No, farther over by that deep area.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:21.090
Adachi plates, 33-9586.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:22.930
That's the one, all right.

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:29.210
It's towards 2 o'clock.

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:32.400
Use your fins... don't kick up any mud with the jets.

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:37.780
Damn! I can't see a thing.

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:40.720
This pattern's not from a machine.

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:44.120
It's big. It's gotta be 12, maybe 13 meters!

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:51.250
It's still closing in!

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:09.870
An Unknown Creature In Tokyo Bay

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:12.210
Takes
Out
Constru-
-ction
Labor
In
One
Shot

00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:13.630
Monster

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:16.750
"Unknown sea creature in Tokyo Bay," huh?

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:21.820
But a <i>sea</i> monster? These sure are strange times we live in.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:23.260
Noa!

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:26.760
It's calling! Tokyo Bay is calling Alphonse!

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:28.700
What's with <i>you?</i>

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:33.430
I'm gonna go get the OK to use the riot gun.

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:37.100
Are these cool times to be living in or what?

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:40.370
We've got giant robots walking around, sea monsters popping up.

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:41.780
Nonsense.

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:44.710
China's interior lakes or Loch Ness, maybe, but <i>Tokyo Bay?</i>

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:47.840
What would a sea monster be doing in a boring place like that?

00:10:48.250 --> 00:10:50.880
But if it isn't a sea monster, what on earth...

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:53.020
It's a whale.

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:56.020
That was no whale, that's for sure.

00:10:56.530 --> 00:11:01.160
A Buchiyama Heavy Industries Nautilus 9800 Marine Labor...

00:11:01.270 --> 00:11:04.790
isn't so flimsy that a <i>whale</i> could take it out.

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:06.630
I was at the controls... <i>I</i> know.

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:10.510
It was down there, 50 meters below the surface.

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:15.280
It was careful, coming at us hidden in a cloud of kicked-up mud.

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:18.510
But I <i>knew</i> it was out there.

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:19.680
Right.

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:22.910
It had to be an advanced creature, one evolving towards life on land...

00:11:23.020 --> 00:11:26.120
evolved from the exceedingly rare aquatic reptiles that lived...

00:11:26.230 --> 00:11:28.520
from the Jurassic Period through the Cetaceous.

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:30.890
<i>I</i> saw it! It was a...

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:34.460
Shige! There's a package here that's addressed to you!

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:35.990
I'm coming!

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:38.270
Sorry about that!

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:43.840
What're you gonna do with this dry ice?
Become an ice cream man?

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:45.500
No, I uh...

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:52.110
So that's the way it is.

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:56.920
If it's really a sea monster, it's way out of our league.

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:00.260
We don't have any handcuffs that big down at HQ.

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:02.220
We don't have 'em <i>here,</i> either.

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:04.660
- "Matsui," was it?
- Right.

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:08.130
Don't you think we'd be better off calling in the Self-Defense Forces?

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:10.070
No, that wouldn't be a good idea.

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:13.610
It'd set a precedent for, you know, dispatching them to keep the peace...

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:15.770
and that kind of thing.

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:17.540
So, um, would you mind?

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:21.440
Why not? This sounds like it's right up the Second Unit's alley.

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:22.810
You should do it.

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:24.820
I don't know what the Chief would say about this.

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:29.580
He'll okay <i>anything.</i> If you're going, I can pass it along to him.

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:37.310
<i>Patlabor</i>

00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:42.320
<i>Patlabor</i>

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:46.410
And now, in regards to the destruction of a Labor...

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:49.000
that was conducting an investigation in Tokyo Bay...

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:52.740
the government announced that they couldn't help but tentatively...

00:12:52.850 --> 00:12:55.480
provisionally, acknowledge the existence of an unidentified...

00:12:55.590 --> 00:12:57.320
creature in Tokyo Bay.

00:12:57.860 --> 00:12:59.980
This has been the 12 o'clock news.

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:02.820
So do you really think it's a sea monster?

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:05.160
It's a <i>whale,</i> I'm telling you.

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:07.600
The disappearance of a lover... Fishing areas drying up...

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:09.220
A destroyed underwater Labor...

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:11.230
Heck, with all that...

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:14.430
all we need to make the story complete is one of <i>them.</i>

00:13:15.070 --> 00:13:17.870
- Yeah! A mad scientist!
- Yep.

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:19.670
Hi.

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:24.040
Give me a mackerel combo with some fried Vienna sausages.

00:13:24.280 --> 00:13:25.510
And a large rice.

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:26.810
Coming right up.

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:33.830
Hirata Biotechnology Laboratory

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:55.110
The "Pan-spermia" Theory?

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:56.510
You've never heard of it?

00:13:56.620 --> 00:14:00.680
It was a theory that had to do with the origin of life on Earth, right?

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:03.780
It began with Svante Arrheniuso, a Swedish Nobel Prize-winning...

00:14:03.890 --> 00:14:07.760
scientist, who proposed that life on Earth originated from...

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:11.960
spores or virus-like micro-organisms from space.

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:14.500
It came to be called the Pan-spermia Theory.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:18.040
In the 1920's, it fell into disrepute under the criticism...

00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:21.590
of Oparin and others, but it's now undergoing a revival.

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:23.910
Was I close?

00:14:24.710 --> 00:14:27.770
I proved it.

00:14:29.450 --> 00:14:34.780
Yes, it was. It's been ten years now.

00:14:36.860 --> 00:14:39.820
One of Oparin's objections was that it was unthinkable that...

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:43.220
the seeds of life could safely reach the surface of the Earth...

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:46.260
after being assaulted by long exposure to cosmic rays...

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:49.090
and the intense heat of entry into Earth's atmosphere.

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:51.070
But <i>meteorites...</i>

00:14:51.170 --> 00:14:53.660
Many organic substances exist inside meteorites...

00:14:53.770 --> 00:14:56.610
<i>especially</i> within carbonaceous chondrite meteorites.

00:14:56.710 --> 00:15:00.110
And so I wondered if I could discover surviving cells...

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:04.110
or cells in a state of suspended animation, within a meteorite.

00:15:04.750 --> 00:15:06.620
And then you found it!

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:11.450
It was within a meteorite recovered from...

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:14.020
a stratum dating back 450 million years.

00:15:14.660 --> 00:15:17.390
It's a strange signal, don't you think?

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:22.230
The Cambrian Period was 450 million years ago...

00:15:22.330 --> 00:15:27.270
during which there was an explosive appearance of species on Earth.

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:32.670
We call this The Big Bang of Life. The Cambrian Explosion.

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:35.940
Um... Are you sure it really came from the meteorite?

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:39.240
Yeah, maybe it got stuck there by accident during the experiment.

00:15:39.350 --> 00:15:42.320
I am Doctor Hirata! I make no mistakes!

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:46.350
I washed my hands with cresol soap before the experiment!

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:50.390
But to err on the side of caution, I performed further experiments.

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:52.550
I pushed genetic engineering to its limits...

00:15:52.660 --> 00:15:55.570
which were constantly being pushed back at that time.

00:15:55.670 --> 00:15:59.830
I hoped to use those cells to trace the path of evolution on Earth.

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:02.370
I split the cells' nuclei, repeatedly performing...

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:05.410
genetic manipulation via chemical agents and radiation...

00:16:05.510 --> 00:16:10.040
cultivating those with promise through alternation of generations...

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:12.340
and then manipulating <i>those</i> genes.

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:17.310
Years passed before I produced anything that could be called "life."

00:16:17.990 --> 00:16:21.360
But it definitely began to evolve.

00:16:21.460 --> 00:16:23.190
I forgot about eating and sleeping...

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:25.920
spurring on this new life-form's evolution.

00:16:26.030 --> 00:16:27.620
I cut back on my sleep...

00:16:27.730 --> 00:16:30.600
abstained from the mackerel combos that I liked so much...

00:16:30.700 --> 00:16:33.360
and threw myself into the experiments!

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:04.790
Eight years.

00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:08.960
After eight long years, it set out on the path to the great reptiles.

00:17:09.070 --> 00:17:10.130
But...

00:17:12.910 --> 00:17:16.110
I was shocked to find that after those countless experiments...

00:17:16.210 --> 00:17:19.210
it had actually <i>learned</i> how to evolve!

00:17:19.320 --> 00:17:22.580
Its evolution not only accelerated, but proceeded at such a speed...

00:17:22.690 --> 00:17:25.590
that I couldn't hope to control it.

00:17:25.690 --> 00:17:28.390
It slipped out of my grasp and became a monster!

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:31.220
The sea water in Tokyo Bay, polluted with waste liquids...

00:17:31.330 --> 00:17:34.230
probably brought about further mutations.

00:17:34.670 --> 00:17:38.360
It's clear that it's grown into an aquatic reptile.

00:17:38.470 --> 00:17:39.600
But that's not all.

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:42.540
If it's grown to the point where it can reproduce on its <i>own...</i>

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:44.300
rather than through cloning in a lab...

00:17:44.410 --> 00:17:47.780
It will continue to mature, and then lay eggs!

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:48.850
Eggs?

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:51.580
The offspring hatched from those eggs will grow...

00:17:51.680 --> 00:17:53.510
mature, and then lay more eggs!

00:17:53.620 --> 00:17:56.780
Their numbers then increase, and still more eggs are laid!

00:17:56.890 --> 00:18:00.050
The oceans of the world will overflow with its kind...

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:04.250
and then they'll march towards land, and... and then...

00:18:05.530 --> 00:18:06.900
It's the end of mankind!

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:10.960
Mankind will become its food! It's the end of the world!

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:21.310
What kind of horror have I unleashed?

00:18:21.550 --> 00:18:24.210
But how did it get into Tokyo Bay?

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:25.280
Well, I...

00:18:27.250 --> 00:18:30.090
You got scared and threw it away.

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:38.790
Bullets! Get me all the bullets we've got!

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:44.340
Okay! Once you get the shells loaded, put it on the trailer!

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:45.930
You got the okay to use it?

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:47.900
I'll take full responsibility.

00:18:48.340 --> 00:18:51.900
Since we know it's not a whale, there's no need to hold back.

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:56.570
Make mincemeat out of that disgusting, egg-laying sea monster...

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:58.480
and feed it to the fishes!

00:18:58.780 --> 00:19:02.050
But you'll only fire on <i>my</i> orders. Got it?

00:19:02.950 --> 00:19:04.350
Yes, ma'am.

00:19:08.590 --> 00:19:12.830
To all cars: The SV2, Second Unit will now leave...

00:19:12.930 --> 00:19:16.060
to exterminate the unidentified sea creature in Tokyo Bay.

00:19:57.280 --> 00:19:58.670
Go for it!

00:19:58.780 --> 00:20:00.510
Hey! Where's Shige?

00:20:00.610 --> 00:20:04.740
Shige? He left a while ago, holding a package really carefully.

00:20:04.850 --> 00:20:06.150
Hey, wait up!

00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:09.190
Don't tell me he... That <i>idiot!</i>

00:20:48.660 --> 00:20:49.820
Hello.

00:20:50.160 --> 00:20:52.220
- Thanks for your help.
- Will it come?

00:20:52.330 --> 00:20:57.890
It'll come, no doubt about it. Animals have a homing instinct.

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:02.130
It'll try to go up the Sumida River to spawn.

00:21:02.340 --> 00:21:03.900
Just like a salmon, huh?

00:21:04.010 --> 00:21:05.030
It's coming!

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:07.380
200 meters from the Takeshiba Pier!

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:10.640
It's heading for shore at a depth of 10 meters!

00:21:15.020 --> 00:21:18.250
Hey, wait! I never said you could use the riot gun, Kanuka!

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:20.290
- It's okay.
- What?

00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:23.590
Noa! The second you catch sight of it, cut loose!

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:26.130
Aren't you scared that we'll kill it?

00:21:26.230 --> 00:21:29.200
Who cares? I want to see it go berserk!

00:21:29.300 --> 00:21:30.360
Hey, now.

00:21:35.170 --> 00:21:37.840
Still closing in! Estimated length, 15 meters!

00:21:37.940 --> 00:21:39.030
Still closing!

00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:40.910
Huh?

00:21:41.380 --> 00:21:42.570
What happened?

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:44.080
I lost the target.

00:21:44.180 --> 00:21:47.350
It must have dived. There are countless holes...

00:21:47.450 --> 00:21:49.940
in the sea floor around here that it could hide in.

00:21:50.050 --> 00:21:51.890
So I guess we have to wait.

00:21:51.990 --> 00:21:54.290
You always stand watch until your target shows up.

00:21:54.390 --> 00:21:55.860
That's a rule of thumb for investigations.

00:21:56.430 --> 00:22:01.870
And so the night of our long, hard struggle began.

00:22:02.630 --> 00:22:06.730
But we didn't just wait idly with our arms folded.

00:22:06.840 --> 00:22:09.430
We marshaled our brains and guts...

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:11.810
and resolutely answered the challenge!

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:15.850
The High-Voltage Current Plan

00:22:16.750 --> 00:22:19.310
Run a high-voltage current through the mouth of the river?

00:22:19.520 --> 00:22:23.610
True, if we took the batteries from your carriers and squad cars...

00:22:23.720 --> 00:22:27.750
and ran them in a series with the local street vendors' generators...

00:22:27.860 --> 00:22:31.760
and pass it through a Labor's condenser, releasing it in one burst...

00:22:31.860 --> 00:22:36.530
we could theoretically produce a million-volt electric current.

00:22:36.630 --> 00:22:39.230
It would be possible to capture it alive.

00:22:39.340 --> 00:22:40.300
What a great idea!

00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:45.140
However, it would probably kill all the fish within several kilometers...

00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:49.200
and would critically damage any undersea cables.

00:22:49.310 --> 00:22:50.750
We'd better not.

00:22:51.140 --> 00:22:55.140
The High-Frequency Underwater
Sound Wave Plan

00:22:56.050 --> 00:22:57.820
Draw it out with high-frequency sound waves?

00:22:57.920 --> 00:23:00.980
Yes, sir! If we use the amps for the Labors' external speakers...

00:23:01.090 --> 00:23:03.150
to amplify the sound waves of the underwater radar...

00:23:03.260 --> 00:23:05.630
I'm certain that it will do the job.

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:09.170
It's true that certain species of insects, including cockroaches...

00:23:09.270 --> 00:23:14.000
and grasshoppers, react and are drawn to certain sound frequencies.

00:23:14.270 --> 00:23:15.300
What a great idea!

00:23:15.410 --> 00:23:19.810
However, it's also widely known that sounds of certain wavelengths...

00:23:19.910 --> 00:23:22.880
have the effect of accelerating egg-laying in aquatic reptiles.

00:23:23.380 --> 00:23:25.320
We'd better not.

00:23:25.420 --> 00:23:29.420
The Tearful Persuasion Plan

00:23:29.690 --> 00:23:31.480
<i>Reason</i> with the sea monster?

00:23:32.220 --> 00:23:34.190
But who would do it?

00:23:34.290 --> 00:23:35.520
It doesn't have a mother or...

00:23:35.630 --> 00:23:37.600
an old high school teacher that we can bring in.

00:23:37.700 --> 00:23:41.890
It has a parent who gave birth to it and raised it: Professor Hirata.

00:23:42.100 --> 00:23:45.870
If he put some love into it, sea monster or not, I <i>know</i> it'd...

00:23:45.970 --> 00:23:49.130
If its brain has progressed to that stage...

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:52.470
it would certainly be possible to communicate with it.

00:23:52.580 --> 00:23:56.450
But what if it harbors misgivings about its own existence...

00:23:56.550 --> 00:23:58.810
and curses its misshapen body?

00:23:58.920 --> 00:24:02.150
Seeing me, the man who gave it life, may reawaken dark memories...

00:24:02.250 --> 00:24:05.650
of being abandoned, making it go insane with rage.

00:24:07.830 --> 00:24:10.590
I'm all for it! I don't care what happens to me!

00:24:10.700 --> 00:24:14.150
But if it turns into a creature of hatred and comes ashore...

00:24:14.270 --> 00:24:18.030
there's no telling <i>what</i> it might do! We must think of the consequences!

00:24:18.140 --> 00:24:22.660
I'm all for it, but think of humanity! Think of the Earth!

00:24:23.880 --> 00:24:28.940
And so, just when it looked like all our ideas had been rejected...

00:24:30.050 --> 00:24:32.610
A small craft has entered the security zone!

00:24:34.490 --> 00:24:35.580
That's...

00:24:36.150 --> 00:24:42.060
It was the genius engineer Shigeo Shiba, the pride of the SV2.

00:24:42.160 --> 00:24:46.260
And the dimly glowing object he held resolutely in his arms...

00:24:47.330 --> 00:24:51.130
The invention of the Devil: a dissolved oxygen destroying device!

00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:53.640
An Oxygen Destroyer!

00:24:54.410 --> 00:24:55.700
What?

00:24:56.270 --> 00:24:58.640
So the day to use this has finally come.

00:24:58.740 --> 00:25:00.010
Shige!

00:25:00.710 --> 00:25:03.700
I thought you'd try something like this, so I tailed you!

00:25:03.820 --> 00:25:04.980
What's with that get-up?

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:06.980
Let me go down there, Chief!

00:25:07.090 --> 00:25:10.250
What's a pipe with dry ice in it supposed to do?

00:25:10.360 --> 00:25:12.480
And you call yourself an engineer?

00:25:12.590 --> 00:25:15.620
- Let me go, Chief! Let me go!
- Idiot!

00:25:28.210 --> 00:25:29.230
It's coming!

00:25:29.340 --> 00:25:31.570
The sea monster is rising rapidly and coming this way!

00:25:50.930 --> 00:25:52.490
H-Hiromi?

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:54.350
What's the matter? Open fire!

00:25:54.470 --> 00:25:57.130
But... But it's...

00:26:01.210 --> 00:26:04.470
I can't believe that it's evolved so far!

00:26:06.740 --> 00:26:09.040
Yamazaki! Where are you going, Yamazaki?

00:26:09.150 --> 00:26:10.380
Yamazaki!

00:26:27.770 --> 00:26:30.760
It was a strange sight, indeed.

00:26:31.370 --> 00:26:34.930
Was it some horrible coincidence? A prank played by God?

00:26:35.140 --> 00:26:39.240
Heaven only knows what exchange of souls passed between those two...

00:26:39.340 --> 00:26:44.680
who stared at each other like they were looking at themselves.

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:48.410
A little time passed, and then a lot.

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:54.820
It's leaving.

00:26:54.930 --> 00:26:56.860
No, it's going home.

00:26:56.960 --> 00:27:01.260
Back to the depths of the sea, the homeland of its soul.

00:27:05.440 --> 00:27:10.900
It was a dramatic, and all too unsatisfying, end to the story.

00:27:11.440 --> 00:27:13.710
The giant sea monster turned its back to us...

00:27:13.810 --> 00:27:16.610
and disappeared into Tokyo Bay's dawn.

00:27:17.550 --> 00:27:19.040
Several days later...

00:27:19.150 --> 00:27:24.880
after it was seen heading south into the Pacific...

00:27:24.990 --> 00:27:29.820
by an American recon plane, it vanished, never to reappear.

00:27:29.960 --> 00:27:33.950
Looking back on it, we never even had a chance to give him a name.

00:27:34.200 --> 00:27:38.070
It was almost like a dream.

00:27:45.350 --> 00:27:49.980
The 450 Million Year Old Trap

00:28:46.740 --> 00:28:49.370
The Labors' Den: A training school for Labor unit members.

00:28:49.470 --> 00:28:51.810
When ordered there for re-training, we saw...

00:28:51.910 --> 00:28:54.430
a ghostly Labor wandering the mountains...

00:28:54.550 --> 00:28:57.110
as well as a reawakening of a blood-soaked tragedy from the past!

00:28:57.210 --> 00:28:58.910
This is all kinda scary, don't you think?

00:28:59.020 --> 00:29:00.980
Next time! "The Tragedy of L"!

00:29:01.090 --> 00:29:02.920
Target, lock on!
